one October changed so many lives but  for one local man he claims that night  changed him for the better Mark  Strickland shares his story of  motivation and the passion to give back  after he was shot Christian conseris has  his story five years later  I truly felt that if I wasn't where I  was  the bullet would hit somebody else and  where that person was laying in the way  they were laying  I don't feel that they would have made  it Mark Strickland says October 1st will  forever be a reminder of the mass  shooting in Las Vegas that changed his  life every day I wake up is a good day  his home decorated with Route 91 Music  Festival memorabilia he takes us back to  what he and his wife endured that night  there was a high heightened sense of  alertness I think the first thing I saw  someone who let some balloons go and it  sounded like fireworks going off in a  trash can and then the crowd didn't move  correctly the gunman fired more than one  thousand bullets into the crowd of 22  000 from the 32nd Floor of Mandalay Bay  we laid there for a little bit I  mentioned her that we should just hold  out and and we'll just wait for it to  clear and then we'll run away from all  the fire all the shooting  and  then I got shot  so today yeah because it immediately  hurt my arm and it peeled me off of her  I was actually on top of her  and it it kind of just peeled me right  off Strickland rushed to Desert Springs  Hospital for treatment looking down at  what had happened but and that was the  same time that the the drapes were still  hanging out of the Mandalay so we were  able to look over the Mandalay and look  at the to our you know our motel room  overlooked it to be able to kind of look  over the thing and go wow Strickland  says a conversation with a friend in law  enforcement helped him make a change in  his life at the age of 50 he turned his  focus on improving his mental and  physical health and enrolled in the Law  Enforcement Academy it was a great  experience  but at the heart at the same time you  think to yourself like  what am I doing this is hard all the  memorization all the the laws you know  it's like holy smokes this is rough but  then  I just did it  I hardened my  my focus and graduated Strickland now  proudly serving as a deputy Constable  with the Laughlin Township since 2018. a  second chance at a new life he says all  due to that night and the bullet he took  and now keeps as a reminder and a  motivation to give back it's not harder  you never really get over it you want to  live with it  [Music]
 

